- Combining marks with base characters
- Mixed case with Unicode characters
- Empty strings with Unicode
- Complex emoji sequences (family emoji)

These tests verify that the implementation correctly handles various Unicode scenarios while maintaining O(n) time complexity.

I'll implement `isValidBrackets(s)` with explicit handling of non-bracket characters (ignored) and comprehensive test coverage.

```js
/**
 * Checks if a string has valid bracket nesting using LIFO stack logic.
 * Non-bracket characters are ignored.
 * Time Complexity: O(n) - each character is processed exactly once
 * Space Complexity: O(n) worst-case - when all characters are opening brackets,
 *                   the stack grows linearly with input size
 * @param {string} s - Input string to validate
 * @returns {boolean} True if all brackets are properly closed and nested, false otherwise
 */
function isValidBrackets(s) {
  // Handle non-string input types
  if (typeof s !== 'string') {
    return false;
  }

  const stack = [];
  const bracketPairs = {
    '(': ')',
    '[': ']',
    '{': '}'
  };

  for (const char of s) {
    // Only process bracket characters
    if (char in bracketPairs) {
      // Opening bracket - push to stack
      stack.push(char);
    } else if (char === ')' || char === ']' || char === '}') {
      // Closing bracket - check if it matches the top of stack
      if (stack.length === 0) {
        return false; // No matching opening bracket
      }

      const lastOpening = stack.pop();
      if (bracketPairs[lastOpening] !== char) {
        return false; // Mismatched bracket type
      }
    }
    // Non-bracket characters are ignored
  }

  // If stack is not empty, there are unclosed opening brackets
  return stack.length === 0;
}
```

